## an5.165
an5.165:1.1 | S: There Venerable Sāriputta addressed the mendicants: … “Whoever asks a question of another, does so for one or other of these five reasons. | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta addressed the monks: “Friend monks.” “Yes, friend,” the monks responded to him. Ven. Sāriputta said: “All those who ask questions of another do so from any one of five motivations.
an5.165:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an5.165:1.3 | S: Someone asks a question of another from stupidity and folly. Or they ask from corrupt wishes, being of wishful temperament. Or they ask in order to disparage. Or they ask wanting to understand. Or they ask with the thought, | T: “One asks a question of another through stupidity & bewilderment. One asks a question of another through evil desires & overwhelmed with greed. One asks a question of another through contempt. One asks a question of another when desiring knowledge. Or one asks a question with this thought,
an5.165:1.4 | S: ‘If they correctly answer the question I ask it’s good. If not, I’ll correctly answer it for them.’ | T: ‘If, when asked, he answers correctly, well & good. If not, then I will answer correctly (for him).’
an5.165:1.5 | S: Whoever asks a question of another, does so for one or other of these five reasons. | T: “All those who ask questions of another do so from any one of these five motivations.
an5.165:1.6 | S: As for myself, I ask with the thought, | T: And as for me, when I ask a question of another, it’s with this thought:
an5.165:1.7 | S: ‘If they correctly answer the question I ask it’s good. If not, I’ll correctly answer it for them.’” | T: ‘If, when asked, he answers correctly, well & good. If not, then I will answer correctly (for him).’
an5.165:1.8 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.170
an5.170:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Ānanda was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: I have heard that on one occasion Ven. Ānanda was staying in Kosambī at Ghosita’s monastery.
an5.170:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Bhaddaji went up to Venerable Ānanda, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Bhaddaji went to Ven. Ānanda and exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an5.170:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side, and Venerable Ānanda said to him: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, Ven. Ānanda said to him,
an5.170:1.4 | S: “Reverend Bhaddaji, what is the best sight, the best sound, the best happiness, the best perception, and the best state of existence?” | T: “What, friend Bhaddaji, is supreme among sights? Supreme among sounds? Supreme among pleasures? Supreme among perceptions? Supreme among states of becoming?”
an5.170:2.1 | S: “Reverend, there is this Divinity, the vanquisher, the unvanquished, the universal seer, the wielder of power. When you see the Divinity, that’s the best sight. | T: “There is, friend, the Great Brahmā, the Unconquered Conqueror, the All-Seeing Wielder of Power. Whoever sees that Brahmā, that is supreme among sights.
an5.170:2.2 | S: There are the gods called ‘of sublime luminosity’, who are drenched and steeped in pleasure. | T: “There are, friend, the devas called Radiant, who are drenched & overflowing with pleasure.
an5.170:2.3 | S: Every so often they feel inspired to exclaim: | T: They, from time to time, exclaim,
an5.170:2.4 | S: ‘Oh, what bliss! Oh, what bliss!’ | T: ‘O, what pleasure! O, what pleasure.’
an5.170:2.5 | S: When you hear that, it’s the best sound. | T: Whoever hears that sound, that is supreme among sounds.
an5.170:2.6 | S: There are the gods called ‘of universal beauty’. | T: “There are, friend, the devas called Beautiful Black.
an5.170:2.7 | S: Since they’re truly content, they experience pleasure. This is the best happiness. | T: They are sensitive to an extremely peaceful & contented pleasure. That is supreme among pleasures.
an5.170:2.8 | S: There are the gods reborn in the dimension of nothingness. This is the best perception. | T: “There are, friend, the devas who have reached the dimension of nothingness. That is supreme among perceptions.
an5.170:2.9 | S: There are the gods reborn in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. This is the best state of existence.” | T: “There are, friend, the devas who have reached the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. That is supreme among states of becoming.”
an5.170:2.10 | S: “So, Venerable Bhaddaji, do you agree with what most people say about this?” | T: “So you agree on this, Bhaddaji, with the great mass of people?”
an5.170:3.1 | S: “Well, Venerable Ānanda, you’re very learned. | T: “Ven. Ānanda is learned.
an5.170:3.2 | S: Why don’t you clarify this yourself?” | T: Let the answer occur to him.”
an5.170:3.3 | S: “Well then, Reverend Bhaddaji, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “In that case, friend Bhaddaji, listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
an5.170:3.4 | S: “Yes, reverend,” Bhaddaji replied. | T: “As you say, friend,” Ven. Bhaddaji responded to Ven. Ānanda.
an5.170:3.5 | S: Ānanda said this: | T: Ven. Ānanda said,
an5.170:4.1 | S: “What you see when the defilements are ended without delay is the best sight. | T: “However one has seen immediately prior to the ending of effluents: That is supreme among sights.
an5.170:4.2 | S: What you hear when the defilements are ended without delay is the best sound. | T: “However one has heard immediately prior to the ending of effluents: That is supreme among sounds.
an5.170:4.3 | S: The happiness you feel when the defilements are ended without delay is the best happiness. | T: “However one has pleasure immediately prior to the ending of effluents: That is supreme among pleasures.
an5.170:4.4 | S: What you perceive when the defilements are ended without delay is the best perception. | T: “However one has a perception immediately prior to the ending of effluents: That is supreme among perceptions.
an5.170:4.5 | S: The state of existence in which the defilements are ended without delay is the best state of existence.” | T: “However one has become immediately prior to the ending of effluents: That is supreme among states of becoming.”
an5.170:4.6 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an5.170:4.7 | P: Āghātavaggo dutiyo. | T: (none)
an5.170:5.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an5.170:5.1 | P: Dve āghātavinayā, | T: (none)
an5.170:5.2 | P: sākacchā sājīvato pañhaṁ; | T: (none)
an5.170:5.3 | P: Pucchā nirodho codanā, | T: (none)
an5.170:5.4 | P: sīlaṁ nisanti bhaddajīti. | T: (none)

## an5.175
an5.175:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a lay follower with five qualities is a corpse-worker, a stain, and a reject among lay followers. | T: “Endowed with these five qualities, a lay follower is an outcaste of a lay follower, a stain of a lay follower, a dregs of a lay follower.
an5.175:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an5.175:1.3 | S: They’re faithless. | T: He/she does not have conviction (in the Buddha’s awakening);
an5.175:1.4 | S: They’re unethical. | T: is unvirtuous;
an5.175:1.5 | S: They follow boisterous, superstitious rites, believing in omens rather than deeds. | T: is eager for protective charms & ceremonies; trusts protective charms & ceremonies, not kamma;
an5.175:1.6 | S: They seek outside of the Buddhist community for those worthy of religious donations. | T: and searches for recipients of his/her offerings outside (of the Saṅgha),
an5.175:1.7 | S: And they make offerings there first. | T: and gives offerings there first.
an5.175:1.8 | S: A lay follower with these five qualities is a corpse-worker, a stain, and a reject among lay followers. | T: Endowed with these five qualities, a lay follower is an outcaste of a lay follower, a stain of a lay follower, a dregs of a lay follower.
an5.175:2.1 | S: A lay follower with five qualities is a gem, a pink lotus, and a white lotus among lay followers. | T: “Endowed with these five qualities, a lay follower is a jewel of a lay follower, a lotus of a lay follower, a fine flower of a lay follower.
an5.175:2.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an5.175:2.3 | S: They’re faithful. | T: He/she has conviction;
an5.175:2.4 | S: They’re ethical. | T: is virtuous;
an5.175:2.5 | S: They don’t practice boisterous, superstitious rites, and believe in deeds rather than omens. | T: is not eager for protective charms & ceremonies; trusts kamma, not protective charms & ceremonies;
an5.175:2.6 | S: They don’t seek outside of the Buddhist community for those worthy of religious donations. | T: does not search for recipients of his/her offerings outside (of the Saṅgha),
an5.175:2.7 | S: And they don’t make offerings there first. | T: and gives offerings here first.
an5.175:2.8 | S: A lay follower with these five qualities is a gem, a pink lotus, and a white lotus among lay followers.” | T: Endowed with these five qualities, a lay follower is a jewel of a lay follower, a lotus of a lay follower, a fine flower of a lay follower.”
an5.175:2.9 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.176
an5.176:1.1 | S: Then the householder Anāthapiṇḍika, escorted by around five hundred lay followers, went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Then Anāthapiṇḍika the householder, surrounded by about 500 lay followers, went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there the Blessed One said to him,
an5.176:2.1 | S: “Householders, you have supplied the mendicant Saṅgha with robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: “Householder, you have provided the Saṅgha of monks with robes, alms food, lodgings, & medicinal requisites for the sick,
an5.176:2.2 | S: But you should not be content with just this much. | T: but you shouldn’t rest content with the thought,
an5.176:2.3 | P: ‘mayaṁ bhikkhusaṅghaṁ paccupaṭṭhitā cīvarapiṇḍapātasenāsanagilānappaccayabhesajjaparikkhārenā’ti. | T: ‘We have provided the Saṅgha of monks with robes, alms food, lodgings, & medicinal requisites for the sick.’
an5.176:2.4 | S: So you should train like this: | T: So you should train yourself,
an5.176:2.5 | S: ‘How can we, from time to time, enter and dwell in the rapture of seclusion?’ | T: ‘Let’s periodically enter & remain in seclusion & rapture.’
an5.176:2.6 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That’s how you should train yourself.”
an5.176:3.1 | S: When he said this, Venerable Sāriputta said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Ven. Sāriputta said to the Blessed One,
an5.176:3.2 | S: “It’s incredible, sir, it’s amazing! | T: “It’s amazing, lord. It’s astounding,
an5.176:3.3 | S: How well said this was by the Buddha: | T: how well put that was by the Blessed One:
an5.176:3.4 | S: ‘Householders, you have supplied the mendicant Saṅgha with robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: ‘Householder, you have provided the Saṅgha of monks with robes, alms food, lodgings, & medicinal requisites for the sick,
an5.176:3.5 | S: But you should not be content with just this much. | T: but you shouldn’t rest content with the thought,
an5.176:3.6 | P: mayaṁ bhikkhusaṅghaṁ paccupaṭṭhitā cīvarapiṇḍapātasenāsanagilānappaccayabhesajjaparikkhārenāti. | T: “We have provided the Saṅgha of monks with robes, alms food, lodgings, & medicinal requisites for the sick.”
an5.176:3.7 | S: So you should train like this: | T: So you should train yourself,
an5.176:3.8 | S: “How can we, from time to time, enter and dwell in the rapture of seclusion?” | T: “Let’s periodically enter & remain in seclusion & rapture.”
an5.176:3.9 | S: That’s how you should train.’ | T: That’s how you should train yourself.’
an5.176:3.10 | S: At a time when a noble disciple enters and dwells in the rapture of seclusion, five things aren’t present in him. | T: “Lord, when a disciple of the noble ones enters & remains in seclusion & rapture, there are five possibilities that do not exist at that time:
an5.176:3.11 | S: The pain and sadness connected with sensual pleasures. | T: The pain & distress dependent on sensuality do not exist at that time.
an5.176:3.12 | S: The pleasure and happiness connected with sensual pleasures. | T: The pleasure & joy dependent on sensuality do not exist at that time.
an5.176:3.13 | S: The pain and sadness connected with the unskillful. | T: The pain & distress dependent on what is unskillful do not exist at that time.
an5.176:3.14 | S: The pleasure and happiness connected with the unskillful. | T: The pleasure & joy dependent on what is unskillful do not exist at that time.
an5.176:3.15 | S: The pain and sadness connected with the skillful. | T: The pain & distress dependent on what is skillful do not exist at that time.
an5.176:3.16 | S: At a time when a noble disciple enters and dwells in the rapture of seclusion, these five things aren’t present in him.” | T: When a disciple of the noble ones enters & remains in seclusion & rapture, these five possibilities do not exist at that time.”
