## an5.177
an5.177:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a lay follower should not engage in these five trades. | T: “Monks, a lay follower should not engage in five types of business.
an5.177:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
an5.177:1.3 | S: Trade in weapons, living creatures, meat, intoxicants, and poisons. | T: Business in weapons, business in living beings, business in meat, business in intoxicants, and business in poison.
an5.177:1.4 | S: A lay follower should not engage in these five trades.” | T: “These are the five types of business a lay follower should not engage in.”
an5.177:1.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.180
an5.180:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was wandering in the land of the Kosalans together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was wandering on a tour among the Kosalans with a large Saṅgha of monks.
an5.180:1.2 | S: While traveling along a road the Buddha saw a large sal grove in a certain spot. | T: As he was going along a road, he saw a large Sal forest in a certain place.
an5.180:1.3 | S: He left the road, went to the sal grove, and plunged deep into it. And at a certain spot he smiled. | T: Going down from the road, he went to the Sal forest. On reaching it, he plunged into it and at a certain spot, broke into a smile.
an5.180:2.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to Ven. Ānanda,
an5.180:2.2 | S: “What is the cause, what is the reason why the Buddha smiled? | T: “What is the cause, what is the reason, for the Blessed One’s breaking into a smile?
an5.180:2.3 | S: Realized Ones do not smile for no reason.” | T: It’s not without purpose that Tathāgatas break into smile.”
an5.180:2.4 | S: So Venerable Ānanda said to the Buddha, | T: So he said to the Blessed One,
an5.180:2.5 | S: “What is the cause, what is the reason why the Buddha smiled? | T: “What is the cause, what is the reason, for the Blessed One’s breaking into a smile?
an5.180:2.6 | S: Realized Ones do not smile for no reason.” | T: It’s not without purpose that Tathāgatas break into smile.”
an5.180:3.1 | S: “Once upon a time, Ānanda, there was a city in this spot that was successful and prosperous, populous and full of people. | T: “In this spot, Ānanda, there was once a great city: powerful, prosperous, populous, crowded with people.
an5.180:3.2 | S: And Kassapa, a blessed one, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha, lived supported by that city. | T: And on that city, Kassapa the Blessed One, worthy & rightly self-awakened, dwelled dependent.
an5.180:3.3 | S: He had a lay follower named Gavesī who had not fulfilled all the precepts. | T: Now, Kassapa the Blessed One, worthy & rightly self-awakened, had a lay follower named Gavesin who didn’t practice in full in terms of his virtue.
an5.180:3.4 | S: And the five hundred lay followers who were taught and advised by Gavesī also had not fulfilled all the precepts. | T: But because of Gavesin, there were 500 people who had been inspired to declare themselves lay followers, and yet who also didn’t practice in full in terms of their virtue.
an5.180:3.5 | S: Then Gavesī thought: | T: “Then the thought occurred to Gavesin the lay follower:
an5.180:3.6 | S: ‘I’m the helper, leader, and adviser of these five hundred lay followers, yet neither I nor they have fulfilled the precepts. | T: ‘I am the benefactor of these 500 lay followers, their leader, the one who has inspired them. But I don’t practice in full in terms of my virtue, just as they don’t practice in full in terms of their virtue.
an5.180:3.7 | S: We’re the same, I’m in no way better. | T: In that we’re exactly even; there’s nothing extra (for me).
an5.180:3.8 | S: So let me do better.’ | T: How about something extra!’
an5.180:4.1 | S: Then Gavesī went to those five hundred lay followers and said to them: | T: So he went to the 500 lay followers and on arrival said to them,
an5.180:4.2 | S: ‘From this day forth may the venerables remember me as one who has fulfilled the precepts.’ | T: ‘From today onward I want you to know me as someone who practices in full in terms of his virtue.’
an5.180:4.3 | S: Then those five hundred lay followers thought: | T: “Then the thought occurred to the 500 lay followers:
an5.180:4.4 | S: ‘Master Gavesī is our helper, leader, and adviser, | T: ‘Master Gavesin is our benefactor, our leader, the one who has inspired us.
an5.180:4.5 | S: and now he will fulfill the precepts. | T: He will now practice in full in terms of his virtue.
an5.180:4.6 | S: Why don’t we do the same?’ | T: So why shouldn’t we?’
an5.180:4.7 | S: Then those five hundred lay followers went to Gavesī and said to him: | T: So they went to Gavesin the lay follower and on arrival said to him,
an5.180:4.8 | S: ‘From this day forth may Master Gavesī remember these five hundred lay followers as having fulfilled the precepts.’ | T: ‘From today onward we want Master Gavesin to know the 500 lay followers as people who practice in full in terms of their virtue.’
an5.180:4.9 | S: Then Gavesī thought: | T: “Then the thought occurred to Gavesin the lay follower:
an5.180:4.10 | S: ‘I’m the helper, leader, and adviser of these five hundred lay followers, and both I and they have fulfilled the precepts. | T: ‘I am the benefactor of these 500 lay followers, their leader, the one who has inspired them. I practice in full in terms of my virtue, just as they practice in full in terms of their virtue.
an5.180:4.11 | S: We’re the same, I’m in no way better. | T: In that we’re exactly even; there’s nothing extra (for me).
an5.180:4.12 | S: So let me do better.’ | T: How about something extra!’
an5.180:5.1 | S: Then Gavesī went to those five hundred lay followers and said to them: | T: So he went to the 500 lay followers and on arrival said to them,
an5.180:5.2 | S: ‘From this day forth may the venerables remember me as one who is chaste, set apart, refraining from the vulgar act of sex.’ | T: ‘From today onward I want you to know me as someone who practices the celibate life, the life apart, abstaining from intercourse, the act of villagers.’

