an5.180:7.1 | S: Then the lay follower Gavesī went up to the blessed one Kassapa, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha and said to him: | T: “So he went to Kassapa the Blessed One, worthy & rightly self-awakened, and on arrival said to him,
an5.180:7.2 | S: ‘Sir, may I receive the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence?’ | T: ‘Lord, may I receive the Going-forth in the Blessed One’s presence. May I receive the Acceptance.’
an5.180:7.3 | S: And he received the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence. | T: So he received the Going-forth in the presence of Kassapa the Blessed One, worthy & rightly self-awakened; he received the Acceptance.
an5.180:7.4 | S: Not long after his ordination, the mendicant Gavesī, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. He lived having achieved with his own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: And not long after his Acceptance—dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute—he in no long time reached & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life, for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, directly knowing & realizing it for himself in the here & now.
an5.180:7.5 | S: He understood: ‘Rebirth is ended; the spiritual journey has been completed; what had to be done has been done; there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: He knew: ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.’
an5.180:7.6 | S: And the mendicant Gavesī became one of the perfected. | T: And thus Gavesin the monk became another one of the arahants.
an5.180:8.1 | S: Then those five hundred lay followers thought: | T: “Then the thought occurred to the 500 lay followers:
an5.180:8.2 | S: ‘Master Gavesī is our helper, leader, and adviser, | T: ‘Master Gavesin is our benefactor, our leader, the one who has inspired us.
an5.180:8.3 | S: He has shaved off his hair and beard, dressed in ocher robes, and gone forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: Having shaven off his hair & beard, having put on the ochre robe, he has gone forth from the home life into homelessness.
an5.180:8.4 | S: Why don’t we do the same?’ | T: So why shouldn’t we?’
an5.180:8.5 | S: Then those five hundred lay followers went up to the blessed one Kassapa, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha and said to him: | T: “So they went to Kassapa the Blessed One, worthy & rightly self-awakened, and on arrival said to him,
an5.180:8.6 | S: ‘Sir, may we receive the going forth and ordination in the Buddha’s presence?’ | T: ‘Lord, may we receive the Going Forth in the Blessed One’s presence. May we receive the Acceptance.’
an5.180:8.7 | S: And they did receive the going forth and ordination in the Buddha’s presence. | T: So they received the Going Forth in the presence of Kassapa the Blessed One, worthy & rightly self-awakened; they received the Acceptance.
an5.180:9.1 | S: Then the mendicant Gavesī thought: | T: “Then the thought occurred to Gavesin the monk:
an5.180:9.2 | S: ‘I get the supreme bliss of freedom whenever I want, without trouble or difficulty. | T: ‘I obtain at will—without difficulty, without hardship—this unexcelled bliss of release.
an5.180:9.3 | S: Oh, may these five hundred mendicants do the same!’ | T: O, that these 500 monks may obtain at will—without difficulty, without hardship—this unexcelled bliss of release!’
an5.180:9.4 | S: Then those five hundred mendicants, living alone, withdrawn, diligent, keen, and resolute, soon realized the supreme culmination of the spiritual path in this very life. They lived having achieved with their own insight the goal for which gentlemen rightly go forth from the lay life to homelessness. | T: Then those 500 monks—dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute—in no long time reached & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life, for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness,
an5.180:9.5 | P: brahmacariyapariyosānaṁ diṭṭheva dhamme sayaṁ abhiññā sacchikatvā upasampajja vihariṁsu. | T: directly knowing & realizing it for themselves in the here & now.
an5.180:9.6 | S: They understood: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: They knew: ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.’
an5.180:10.1 | S: And so, Ānanda, those five hundred mendicants headed by Gavesī, trying to go higher and higher, better and better, realized the supreme bliss of freedom. | T: And thus did those 500 monks—headed by Gavesin, striving at what is more & more excellent, more & more refined—realize unexcelled release.
an5.180:10.2 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “So, Ānanda, you should train yourselves:
an5.180:10.3 | S: ‘Trying to go higher and higher, better and better, we will realize the supreme bliss of freedom.’ | T: ‘Striving at what is more & more excellent, more & more refined, we will realize unexcelled release.’
an5.180:10.4 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.”
an5.180:10.5 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an5.180:10.6 | P: Upāsakavaggo tatiyo. | T: (none)
an5.180:11.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an5.180:11.1 | P: Sārajjaṁ visārado nirayaṁ, | T: (none)
an5.180:11.2 | P: Veraṁ caṇḍālapañcamaṁ; | T: (none)
an5.180:11.3 | P: Pīti vaṇijjā rājāno, | T: (none)
